# Secret Env Files

AI agents must not read or print the contents of local secret files in this repository.

Forbidden to read:
- `project/services/bot_service/.env`
- `project/services/auth_service/.env`
- any `.env` file that may contain real local secrets

Allowed:
- read `.env.example` files because they must not contain secrets;
- check whether required keys are empty or set without printing values;
- use commands that report only status such as `MAX_BOT_TOKEN=set` or `OPENROUTER_API_KEY=empty`.

If a task requires changing a real token, ask the user to edit the `.env` file manually unless `env-` mode is active.

## Temporary `env-` Access Mode

The user may explicitly disable the no-read rule for the currently open `.env` file with:

`env-`

Protocol:
1. Treat `env-` as a request to temporarily allow reading and editing only the `.env` file that is open in the active editor window.
2. Before reading anything, identify the active file path in the warning message and wait for the user to confirm with exactly:
   `u`
3. Warning text to show:
   "Warning: before I read this file, mask or remove real MAX and OpenRouter tokens if you do not want the agent to see them. I will work only with this active `.env` file: `<path>`. Confirm masking is complete and the path is correct by replying `u`."
4. Do not read the active `.env` file until the user replies `u`.
5. After `u`, the agent may read and work with only that one active `.env` file across multiple iterations.
6. Do not print real token values in chat. Prefer validation statuses or masked values, for example `MAX_BOT_TOKEN=set` and `OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...abcd`.
7. After each iteration while `env-` mode is active, offer to activate `env+` to restore the no-read rule.
8. If the developer does not respond for more than 3 minutes after work in `env-` mode, treat `env+` as automatically activated at the next opportunity and do not read `.env` files again until a new `env-` flow is confirmed.
9. `env-` does not allow reading other `.env` files, directory-wide secret searches, or unrelated secret files.

## `env+` Restore Mode

`env+` restores the default no-read rule after `env-` mode.

Protocol:
1. When the user sends `env+`, immediately end the active `env-` permission.
2. After `env+`, do not read or edit any `.env` file unless the user starts a new `env-` flow and confirms it with `u`.
3. `env+` is also the automatic fallback after more than 3 minutes of user inactivity during `env-` mode.
